One way of making tins better is to say that making a tin uses only
part of a corpse.  How does a dragon with 1500 nutrition disappear
entirely into a 50-nutrition tin?  (Well, maybe your magic tinning kit
uses some of this energy to do the tinning.  But certainly not all of
it, or you'd never be able to tin anything but dragons.)

What if tinning reduced a corpse by, say, 60 nutrition and a
proportional amount of its weight?  So, a large corpse could be turned
into a large number of tins.  This makes more sense, but there are a
few problems.  One is it changes the food balance of the game quite a
bit.  This may not be such a bad thing, but there's already a huge
food surplus once you pass a certain point in the game.

The other problem is more serious.  It screws up the notion of eating
corpses to gain intrinsics.  If eating one tin can give you an
intrinsic, why do you need to eat the whole corpse before it will give
you the intrinsic?  Turn one minor creature into a dozen tins and you
have a good chance of gaining an intrinsic.

This can be fixed by changing the model of gaining intrinsics.  There
are a couple reasonable approaches to this.
